{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "comment": [
    "What the pack picker must make of the bytes a terminal sends for one keypress.",
    "",
    "Three implementations decode escape sequences by hand — Java, Rust and Python — and",
    "they are the three this table holds. TypeScript hands the job to Node’s readline and C#",
    "to Console.ReadKey, so neither ever sees a raw byte and neither reads this file. That is",
    "also why the expected names are not generated from the TypeScript reference the way every",
    "other fixture is: there is no reference decoder to generate them from. They come from the",
    "escape sequences a terminal actually emits, and the three are held to each other.",
    "",
    "`left` is what must still be unread once the key has been named. That is the half that was",
    "wrong: Java and Rust stopped at a `~` only for the four numbers they knew, so Delete and",
    "Insert named themselves `unknown` and left their `~` in the stream — and the next turn of",
    "the loop read that `~` as a keystroke and typed it into the search box."
  ],
